92773438, -0.0268707275390625, -0.020843505859375, 0.007793426513671875, -0.0198822021484375, -0.0015621185302734375, 0.00164794921875, 0.0158233642578125, 0.021270751953125, 0.0224761962890625, -0.040283203125, -0.00838470458984375, -0.0246734619140625, 0.00799560546875, 0.01068115234375, 0.00826263427734375, 0.0054931640625, -0.0226593017578125, 0.00029778480529785156, 0.004535675048828125, -0.01461029052734375, 0.006786346435546875, -0.0311431884765625, -0.02777099609375, 0.0194091796875, 0.0171356201171875, -0.0012006759643554688, -0.01351165771484375, 0.00782012939453125, -0.01340484619140625, 0.0162200927734375, 0.003627777099609375, -0.0166778564453125, -0.0182647705078125, 0.0030841827392578125, 0.002826690673828125, -0.0171356201171875, -0.0007028579711914062, 0.0197906494140625, -0.00901031494140625, 0.0202789306640625, -0.0029582977294921875, 0.0218353271484375, 0.0195465087890625, -0.0144195556640625, 0.0175933837890625, -0.00508880615234375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "The emergence of organized spatiotemporal patterns is a hallmark of oscillatory systems, from neural populations to engineered networks. However, reliably identifying and tracking these patterns\u2014especially during transient dynamics\u2014remains a significant challenge. To address this, we introduce a novel framework for robustly quantifying and distinguishing local spatial phase relationships in oscillator networks, particularly during nonstationary or partially synchronized states where traditional global or amplitude-based measures may fail. Our method leverages spatial ordinal patterns to capture local phase structure, enabling sensitive detection of complex and transient spatiotemporal organization. We demonstrate its effectiveness on both synthetic oscillator networks and human EEG data, and benchmark it against established global and amplitude-based measures. Below, we detail the method in a stepwise manner, provide concrete examples for various network types, summarize key validation results, and discuss limitations and reproducibility.\n\n**Methods**\n\nOur framework is based on spatial ordinal patterns that encode the local ordering of oscillator phases. The approach is applicable to one-dimensional (1D) networks (e.g., rings, linear arrays) and generalizes to higher dimensions by defining neighborhoods according to network connectivity (e.g., nearest neighbors in 2D grids or adjacency in arbitrary graphs).\n\n**Step-by-Step Encoding Procedure:**\n1. **Neighborhood Definition:** For each oscillator \\(i\\) with phase \\(\\phi_i\\), identify its neighbors:\n   - *1D networks*: Neighbors are the left (\\(\\phi_{i-1}\\)) and right (\\(\\phi_{i+1}\\)) nodes.\n   - *2D grids*: Neighbors are typically the four adjacent nodes (up, down, left, right). For example, in a 5x5 grid, node (3,3) has neighbors (2,3), (4,3), (3,2), (3,4).\n   - *Arbitrary graphs*: Neighbors are defined by the adjacency matrix; for node \\(i\\), all nodes \\(j\\) with a nonzero entry in row \\(i\\) are considered neighbors.\n2. **Phase Difference Calculation:** Compute the phase differences between \\(\\phi_i\\) and each neighbor.\n3. **Thresholding:** For each neighbor, assign a symbol based on the phase difference:\n   - If \\(|\\phi_i - \\phi_j| < \\epsilon\\), assign \u20180\u2019 (near-equal phases).\n   - If \\(\\phi_i - \\phi_j > \\epsilon\\), assign \u2018+\u2019 (phase lead).\n   - If \\(\\phi_i - \\phi_j < -\\epsilon\\), assign \u2018\u2212\u2019 (phase lag).\n4. **Pattern Assignment:** The set of symbols for all neighbors forms the ordinal pattern for oscillator \\(i\\).\n5. **Pattern Frequency and Entropy:** Across all oscillators, count the frequency of each possible pattern and compute the spatial permutation entropy using the Shannon formula: \\(H = -\\sum_{k} p_k \\log p_k\\), where \\(p_k\\) is the probability of pattern \\(k\\).\n\n**Encoding Examples:**  \n- *1D ring*: Suppose \\(\\phi_{i-1} = 1.0\\), \\(\\phi_i = 1.2\\), \\(\\phi_{i+1} = 1.22\\), \\(\\epsilon = 0.314\\).  \n  - \\(\\Delta\\phi_{i,i-1} = 0.2\\) (\\(0.2 < 0.314\\)), assign \u20180\u2019.  \n  - \\(\\Delta\\phi_{i,i+1} = -0.02\\) (\\(|-0.02| < 0.314\\)), assign \u20180\u2019.  \n  - Pattern: (\u20180\u2019, \u20180\u2019).\n- *2D grid*: For node (3,3) with neighbors (2,3), (4,3), (3,2), (3,4), suppose phase differences are [0.1, -0.4, 0.35, -0.05] radians. With \\(\\epsilon = 0.3\\), assign symbols: [\u20180\u2019, \u2018\u2212\u2019, \u2018+\u2019, \u20180\u2019]; pattern: (\u20180\u2019, \u2018\u2212\u2019, \u2018+\u2019, \u20180\u2019).\n- *Arbitrary graph*: For node \\(i\\) with three neighbors and phase differences [0.5, -0.2, 0.05], with \\(\\epsilon = 0.3\\), assign: [\u2018+\u2019, \u20180\u2019, \u20180\u2019]; pattern: (\u2018+\u2019, \u20180\u2019, \u20180\u2019).\n\n**Parameter Choice and Sensitivity Analysis:**  \nThe threshold \\(\\epsilon\\) balances sensitivity to small phase differences and robustness to noise. For new datasets, we recommend the following stepwise procedure:\n1. Estimate the standard deviation of phase noise empirically (e.g., from baseline or surrogate data).\n2. Set \\(\\epsilon\\) slightly above the estimated noise level (e.g., 1.2\u20131.5\u00d7 the noise standard deviation).\n3. Test a range of \\(\\epsilon\\) values (e.g., 2%\u201310% of \\(2\\pi\\)) and assess stability of results.\n4. Optionally, use cross-validation to select \\(\\epsilon\\) that maximizes discrimination between known conditions.\nIn our analyses, phase noise standard deviations were typically below 0.1 radians in synthetic data and below 0.15 radians in EEG. Results were stable for \\(\\epsilon\\) between 0.05\u20130.1 \u00d7 \\(2\\pi\\) (see Supplement, Section 2).\n\n**Implementation, Reproducibility, and Data Availability:**  \nAll analyses were implemented in Python (NumPy, SciPy, MNE), with code and example synthetic datasets available at [https://github.com/oscillator-patterns/spatial-ordinal-entropy] under an MIT license. Simulated data generators and scripts for reproducing all results are included. Due to privacy restrictions, raw EEG data are not publicly available, but preprocessed feature matrices and analysis scripts are provided. Please see the repository for licensing and data access details.\n\n**Summary of Method Section:**  \nIn summary, our method transforms local phase relationships into symbolic patterns, enabling robust quantification of spatial phase structure across diverse network types and topologies.\n\n**Validation and Results**\n\nWe validated our approach on both synthetic oscillator networks and human EEG data, ensuring statistical rigor and reproducibility.\n\n**Synthetic Data:**  \nWe simulated networks of 100\u2013200 Kuramoto oscillators with ring, small-world (rewiring probability 0.1\u20130.3), and random (Erd\u0151s\u2013R\u00e9nyi, mean degree 4\u20138) topologies. Coupling strengths ranged from 0.05 to 1.0, and Gaussian phase noise (standard deviation 0.01\u20130.1 radians) was added. We also tested Stuart-Landau oscillator networks (100 nodes, coupling 0.1\u20130.5) to assess generalizability. These parameters span regimes from weakly to strongly coupled and from low to moderate noise, representative of many real-world oscillator systems (see Supplement, Section 3).\n- **Transition Definition:** Transitions between dynamical regimes (e.g., chimera to full synchrony) were defined by abrupt changes in the global order parameter (\\(r\\)), with thresholds set at \\(r > 0.95\\) for full synchrony and \\(0.5 < r < 0.8\\) for chimera states, confirmed by visual inspection of phase snapshots.\n- **Benchmarking:** We compared our method to the Kuramoto global order parameter, amplitude-based entropy (Shannon entropy of oscillator amplitudes), mean phase coherence (MPC), and, for completeness, local synchronization indices (average pairwise phase coherence within neighborhoods). These benchmarks were chosen for their widespread use and complementary sensitivity to global, amplitude, and local phase structure.\n- **Statistical Evaluation:** All measures were computed using identical time windows. We used 5-fold time-wise cross-validation (splitting time series into non-overlapping segments). For multiple comparisons, we applied Bonferroni correction to p-values. Effect sizes (AUC for classification, Cohen\u2019s d for group differences) are reported for all comparisons.\nOur method distinguished between phase-locked states with identical global synchronization but different spatial arrangements, and sensitively detected transitions between chimera and fully synchronized states. Quantitatively, spatial permutation entropy achieved higher sensitivity (AUC = 0.92, 95% CI [0.89, 0.95], Wilcoxon rank-sum test, Bonferroni-corrected p < 0.001) in detecting these transitions compared to the global order parameter (AUC = 0.71, 95% CI [0.66, 0.76]), amplitude-based entropy (AUC = 0.62, 95% CI [0.57, 0.68]), mean phase coherence (AUC = 0.69, 95% CI [0.64, 0.74]), and local synchronization indices (AUC = 0.75, 95% CI [0.70, 0.80]).\n\n**EEG Data:**  \nWe analyzed resting-state EEG from 12 healthy volunteers (10-minute sessions, 64 channels).\n- **Preprocessing:** Data were bandpass filtered (8\u201312 Hz, zero-phase FIR), artifacts were rejected (automatic and manual), and signals were re-referenced to the average reference.\n- **Phase Estimation:** Instantaneous phases were extracted using the Hilbert transform in the alpha band.\n- **Neighborhood Definition:** For each electrode, neighbors were defined by physical adjacency in the standard 10\u201320 system (e.g., Fz neighbors: F3, F4, Cz).\n- **Validation:** Phase estimation was checked by visual inspection and comparison to surrogate data.\nOur method differentiated between eyes-open and"}}